---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Beyond work"
  description="Tennis, chess, and what two amateur games taught Serhat Akkuş about building software."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Beyond work"
    title="Tennis and chess."
    lede="Both amateur. Both taken seriously. Both, it turns out, teaching me the same lesson from different directions."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="pursuits">
        <article class="pursuit">
          <span class="pursuit__icon" aria-hidden="true">🎾</span>
          <h2 class="pursuit__title">Tennis</h2>
          <p>
            I play regularly, and badly enough to still be improving. Tennis is a game of recovering
            from your last mistake fast enough to play the next ball — which is a skill I would
            recommend to anyone who writes software for a living.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also how{" "}
            <a href="https://tenisplatformu.org" rel="noopener" target="_blank">tenisplatformu.org</a>{" "}
            came about. I joined the Çanakkale tennis community in 2023 and its admin team not long
            after — a community that needed better tooling than a group chat, and I happened to be
            the person in it who could build that. Around 120 players use the app now. Of everything
            on this site, it is the project I am most attached to: software that made a real thing
            better for people I actually see every week.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="pursuit">
          <span class="pursuit__icon" aria-hidden="true">♞</span>
          <h2 class="pursuit__title">Chess</h2>
          <p>
            An amateur, permanently. What keeps me at the board is that chess is completely honest
            with you: there is no bad luck, no unfair bounce, no one else to blame. You lost because
            you missed something. That is an unusual thing to spend your free time volunteering for.
          </p>
          <p>
            The most useful habit chess gave me is checking my opponent's reply before making my own
            move — which in software means asking what breaks, who gets paged, and what this looks
            like in two years, before I commit to the clever solution.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why this is on a professional website</h2>
        <p>
          Because the overlap is real. Both games punish the same mistake — playing the shot you wish
          you had instead of the one actually in front of you — and so does building software. Most
          bad architecture I have seen came from designing for the system somebody hoped to have
          rather than the one they had.
        </p>
        <p>
          And because if you are thinking about working with me, you probably want some sense of who
          you would be talking to every week. This is it: someone who likes games with no luck in
          them, and who will tell you what he actually thinks.
        </p>
        <p><a class="arrow-link" href="/contact/">Say hello</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .pursuits {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    .pursuits {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .pursuit {
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: var(--space-l);
  }

  .pursuit__icon {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    line-height: 1;
    display: block;
  }

  .pursuit__title {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    margin-block: var(--space-xs) var(--space-s);
  }

  .pursuit p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .pursuit p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
  }

  .pursuit a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
